@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

git-ssb put git repositories on Secure Scuttlebutt: a repo is a message,
a push is a message naming the packs it stored as blobs, and issues,
pull requests and comments are messages too.  ssb_git is an erlbutt
plugin that speaks the same formats, so every repo published since 2016
can be cloned, pushed to and discussed again.

The plugin has two front ends.  Git is one: @command{git clone},
@command{git fetch} and @command{git push} work on @code{ssb://} URLs.
ssb-git is the other, for everything git has no word for: browsing the
repos the node holds, reading a repo's pushes, and filing, reading,
commenting on and closing issues and pull requests.

ssb-git does not run git for you, beyond a clone and checking out a
pull request.  Commit, branch and push however you already do; Emacs's
built-in VC (@kbd{C-x v P} pushes) works on an @code{ssb://} remote like
any other.

