@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
A running erlbutt node with the ssb_git plugin started.  erlbutt's
packages ship it; @code{plugin_loader:list().} on the node says whether
it started.

@item
maxbutt, set up and connected: ssb-git uses its Distel connection to the
node (@code{ssb-node}), its feed view and its thread view.  The maxbutt
must be recent enough to carry non-ASCII text correctly (its erlext
UTF-8 fix, October 2026); with an older one, titles and comments outside
ASCII are garbled in both directions.  @xref{Top,,, maxbutt, Maxbutt
User Manual}.

@item
Optionally, @code{markdown-mode}, for compose buffers.  Without it they
use @code{text-mode}.
@end itemize

@node Loading
@section Loading

The plugin carries its own elisp, so load it from the release it runs
in, and the two always match:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "<release>/plugins/ssb_git/elisp")
(require 'ssb-git)
@end lisp

@noindent
@code{<release>} is the erlbutt release directory, such as
@file{~/erlbutt-ssb}.  If you put ssb_git in the node's data directory
instead, load it from @file{<data>/plugins/ssb_git/elisp}.

Nothing needs starting: the first command connects through maxbutt.  If
the node answers but the plugin does not, ssb-git says so: ``is the
ssb_git plugin running in @var{node}?''.

@node Finding git-remote-ssb
@section Finding git-remote-ssb

Git runs @command{git-remote-ssb} for an @code{ssb://} URL, looking for
it on @env{PATH}.  On macOS an Emacs started from the Dock or Finder
gets launchd's @env{PATH}, not your shell's, so a directory such as
@file{~/.local/bin} is not on it, and a clone from Emacs fails with
``git: 'remote-ssb' is not a git command''.

ssb-git avoids this for the git it runs itself: it puts the plugin's
@file{bin/}, beside the @file{elisp/} it was loaded from, first on
@env{PATH}.  @xref{Customization}, @code{ssb-git-bin-directory}.

Git run any other way inside Emacs (VC, @kbd{M-x shell}, @kbd{M-x
compile}) does not go through ssb-git.  For that, add the directory
holding your @command{git-remote-ssb} link to Emacs's own @env{PATH}:

@lisp
(let ((bin (expand-file-name "~/.local/bin")))
  (setenv "PATH" (concat bin path-separator (getenv "PATH")))
  (add-to-list 'exec-path bin))
@end lisp

@node Git on SSB
@chapter Git on SSB

This chapter is about the command line, which ssb-git leaves to git.

A repo's URL is @code{ssb://} followed by its message id:

@example
git clone ssb://%n92DiQh7ietE+R+X/I403LQoyf2DtR3WQfCkDKlheQU=.sha256 git-ssb
@end example

Fetch and push go through the local erlbutt node, never the network
directly.  A clone is read from the packs the node already holds; any it
lacks are asked of its peers, and the fetch waits for them for a time.
A push stores its pack on the node and publishes the update from your
feed.

Commands git has no word for are under @command{git ssb}:

@table @code
@item git ssb create @var{name} [--remote @var{remote}]
Publish a new repo and print its URL; with @option{--remote}, also add
it as a remote of the repository you are in.  Its branches arrive with
the first push.

@item git ssb name @var{name} [--remote @var{remote}]
Rename the repo @var{remote} (by default @code{origin}) points at.  Only
your own repos: a name for someone else's would not count
(@pxref{Whose Word Counts}).

@item git ssb repos [--mine | --author @var{feed}]
List the repos the node knows.  A name in brackets is one someone other
than the repo's author gave it.
@end table

A push closes the issues it fixes, as git-ssb's did: a commit it adds to
the repo's @code{HEAD} branch whose message says, for example,
@samp{fixes %@var{issue id}} closes that issue.  @xref{Whose Word
Counts}.

