@node The Repos List
@section The Repos List

@deffn Command ssb-git-repos &optional mine
List every git repo the node knows, most recently active first: the
date of its last push (or of its creation), its name, its author, and
its open issues and pull requests.  With a prefix argument, only the
repos you created.
@end deffn

A repo's name is the one its author gave it.  A name shown dimmed is one
someone else gave a repo its author never named; a repo with no name at
all shows its short id.  @xref{Whose Word Counts}.

The list is a @code{tabulated-list-mode} buffer: click a column header,
or use @kbd{S}, to sort by it.

@table @kbd
@kindex RET @r{(repos list)}
@item RET
Open the repo at point (@code{ssb-git-open-repo}).
@findex ssb-git-open-repo

@kindex m @r{(repos list)}
@item m
Switch between all repos and only yours (@code{ssb-git-repos-toggle-mine}).
@findex ssb-git-repos-toggle-mine

@kindex g @r{(repos list)}
@item g
Refresh.
@end table

@node A Repo
@section A Repo

@deffn Command ssb-git-repo id
Show repo @var{id}, a @code{git-repo} message id, with or without the
@code{ssb://} prefix.  Also what @kbd{RET} opens from the repos list.
@end deffn

A repo buffer shows, from the top:

@itemize @bullet
@item
its name and URL, who created it and when, and the repo it forks, if
any;

@item
every name it has been given, when one differs from its current name,
each with who gave it; names that are not its author's are marked
``not counted'';

@item
its open issue and pull request counts;

@item
its branches, @samp{*} marking @code{HEAD}.  A branch named like
@code{%@var{update id}/master} is a @dfn{conflict branch}: two people
pushed to @code{master} concurrently, and this is the value that did
not become the plain branch.  It is marked with the branch it competes
for;

@item
its recent pushes, newest first: who pushed, the branches each moved
(or deleted), and the commits each added.
@end itemize

@table @kbd
@kindex i @r{(repo)}
@item i
List the repo's open issues (@code{ssb-git-issues}).
@findex ssb-git-issues

@kindex P @r{(repo)}
@item P
List its open pull requests (@code{ssb-git-pull-requests}).
@findex ssb-git-pull-requests

@kindex N @r{(repo)}
@item N
File a new issue (@code{ssb-git-new-issue}).  @xref{Composing}.
@findex ssb-git-new-issue

@kindex R @r{(repo)}
@item R
Open a pull request (@code{ssb-git-new-pull-request}).  @xref{Composing}.

@kindex w @r{(repo)}
@item w
Copy the repo's @code{ssb://} URL (@code{ssb-git-copy-url}).
@findex ssb-git-copy-url

@kindex C @r{(repo)}
@item C
Clone the repo (@code{ssb-git-clone}).  @xref{Cloning}.

@kindex RET @r{(repo)}
@kindex t @r{(repo)}
@item RET
@itemx t
Open the push at point as a thread in maxbutt's thread view, to read or
reply to the discussion of it (@code{ssb-show-thread}).

@kindex f @r{(repo)}
@item f
Browse the feed of the author at point (@code{ssb-browse-author-feed}).

@kindex n @r{(repo)}
@kindex p @r{(repo)}
@item n
@itemx p
Move to the next or previous push (@code{ssb-git-next-item},
@code{ssb-git-prev-item}).
@findex ssb-git-next-item
@findex ssb-git-prev-item

@kindex g @r{(repo)}
@item g
Refresh.
@end table

@node Cloning
@section Cloning

@deffn Command ssb-git-clone dir
Clone this repo into @var{dir}, which defaults to the repo's name.  Git
runs in the background, its output in the @file{*ssb-git output*}
buffer, which is shown if it fails.
@end deffn

A clone is an ordinary git clone with @code{origin} set to the repo's
@code{ssb://} URL; push to it as you would to any remote.

@node Issues and Pull Requests
@chapter Issues and Pull Requests

An issue and a pull request are the same kind of thing on SSB: a message
naming a repo, with a title and text, whose comments are ordinary posts
replying to it.  So comments made here show up in any SSB client, as
replies in your feed.  A pull request also names a branch of some repo
(often a fork) that it asks to have merged into a branch of this one.

@menu
* Issue Lists::                 A repo's issues or pull requests.
* An Issue::                    Its text, changes and comments.
* Closing and Reopening::       Changing an issue's state.
* Checking Out a Pull Request:: Its branch, in a clone of yours.
@end menu

@node Issue Lists
@section Issue Lists

@kbd{i} and @kbd{P} in a repo buffer list its open issues or pull
requests, newest first.

@table @kbd
@kindex RET @r{(issue list)}
@item RET
Open the issue or pull request at point (@code{ssb-git-open-issue}).
@findex ssb-git-open-issue

@kindex s @r{(issue list)}
@item s
Show open, then closed, then all (@code{ssb-git-issues-cycle-state}).
@findex ssb-git-issues-cycle-state

@kindex N @r{(issue list)}
@item N
File a new issue against the repo (@code{ssb-git-new-issue}).

@kindex g @r{(issue list)}
@item g
Refresh.
@end table

@node An Issue
@section An Issue

@deffn Command ssb-git-issue id
Show issue or pull request @var{id}, a message id.  Also what @kbd{RET}
opens from an issue list.
@end deffn

An issue buffer shows its title, its state (open, closed, or for a pull
request merged), who filed it and on which repo, and for a pull request
the branch it wants merged and where.  Then its text; every change
anyone has published to its state or title, marked ``not counted'' when
it does not count (@pxref{Whose Word Counts}); and its comments, replies
to a comment indented beneath it.

@table @kbd
@kindex r @r{(issue)}
@item r
Comment (@code{ssb-git-comment}).  @xref{Composing}.
@findex ssb-git-comment

@kindex x @r{(issue)}
@item x
Close it if open, reopen it if closed (@code{ssb-git-toggle-open}).
@xref{Closing and Reopening}.

@kindex o @r{(issue)}
@item o
Check out a pull request's branch (@code{ssb-git-checkout-pull-request}).
@xref{Checking Out a Pull Request}.

@kindex t @r{(issue)}
@item t
Open the comment thread in maxbutt's thread view (@code{ssb-git-thread}),
with all of maxbutt's thread commands.
@findex ssb-git-thread

@kindex f @r{(issue)}
@item f
Browse the feed of the author at point (@code{ssb-browse-author-feed}).

@kindex n @r{(issue)}
@kindex p @r{(issue)}
@item n
@itemx p
Move to the next or previous comment or change.

@kindex g @r{(issue)}
@item g
Refresh.
@end table

@node Closing and Reopening
@section Closing and Reopening

@deffn Command ssb-git-toggle-open
Close this issue or pull request if it is open, reopen it if it is
closed, after asking.  This publishes an @code{issue-edit} message from
your feed, which cannot be taken back; reopening is a new message, not
an undo.
@end deffn

Anyone can publish a close, but it counts only from the issue's author
or the repo's owner.  If you are neither, yours is published and shown
as not counted.  @xref{Whose Word Counts}.

@node Checking Out a Pull Request
@section Checking Out a Pull Request

@deffn Command ssb-git-checkout-pull-request dir
Fetch this pull request's branch from the repo it names into the clone
in @var{dir}, as the branch @code{pr/@var{id}} (the first characters of
the pull request's id), and check it out.  @var{dir} defaults to the
clone you are in.
@end deffn

Review it, merge it with git as you like, and push.  A push that brings
the pull request's branch tip into the repo's @code{HEAD} branch marks
it merged, as git-ssb's did; like any change of state, that counts when
the pusher is the repo's owner or the pull request's author.

@node Composing
@chapter Composing

New issues, pull requests and comments are written in the
@file{*ssb-git compose*} buffer, in @code{markdown-mode} if you have it.
The shaded lines at the top say what you are writing and are not sent.

For an issue or a pull request, the first line is the title; then a
blank line, then the text.  The title is also put at the top of the
text, so clients that know nothing of git, such as Silkpurse or Patchwork,
show it.  A comment has no title.

@table @kbd
@kindex C-c C-c @r{(compose)}
@item C-c C-c
Publish (@code{ssb-git-compose-send}).  Publishing cannot be undone.
@findex ssb-git-compose-send

@kindex C-c C-k @r{(compose)}
@item C-c C-k
Discard, after asking (@code{ssb-git-compose-cancel}).
@findex ssb-git-compose-cancel
@end table

@deffn Command ssb-git-new-pull-request head-repo head-branch branch
Ask for @var{head-branch} of @var{head-repo} to be merged into this
repo's @var{branch}.  @var{head-repo} defaults to this repo, and
@var{branch} to its @code{HEAD}.  Push the branch first: a pull request
names a branch its readers can already fetch.
@end deffn

@node Whose Word Counts
@chapter Whose Word Counts

On SSB nobody can stop anyone publishing anything, so who may do what is
decided by the reader.  ssb_git's rules:

@table @asis
@item An issue's state
Changes count from the issue's author and from the repo's owner (the
author of its @code{git-repo} message); among those, the latest wins.
Others' are kept and shown, marked ``not counted''.

@item A repo's name
Its author's latest @code{about} name for it, else the name in its
@code{git-repo} message.  Names anyone else gave it are kept: they are
shown dimmed for a repo its author never named, and listed in the repo
buffer.

@item A push closing issues
A commit added to the repo's @code{HEAD} branch whose message says
@samp{close}, @samp{closes}, @samp{closed}, @samp{closing}, @samp{fix},
@samp{fixes}, @samp{fixed} or @samp{fixing}, optionally @samp{issue},
then an issue's id, closes that issue.  Whether it counts follows the
rule for any state change: so a push by the repo's owner or the issue's
author.

@item Branches
Anyone can push.  When pushes conflict, one value becomes the plain
branch and the others conflict branches (@pxref{A Repo}), as git-ssb
decided it.
@end table

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

@defopt ssb-git-bin-directory
@vindex ssb-git-bin-directory
The directory holding @command{git-remote-ssb}, put first on @env{PATH}
for the git ssb-git runs (a clone, a pull request checkout).  By default
the plugin's @file{bin/}, beside the @file{elisp/} this file was loaded
from.  @code{nil} leaves @env{PATH} alone.  @xref{Finding
git-remote-ssb}.
@end defopt

@defopt ssb-node
@vindex ssb-node
maxbutt's: the Erlang node name of the erlbutt node, as a symbol.
ssb-git talks to the same node.  If maxbutt commands work and ssb-git's
say the plugin is not running, check the plugin on that node.
@end defopt

